### Story "The Staff Room", chapter "The Stuck Queue": Monday morning,
### nothing prints. A stopped queue, a 900-page job at its head, the spool
### filling /var. lpstat, lpq, the error_log, cancel, cupsenable.
### Commands, options, paths and outputs are not translated.

chapter-stuck-queue = The Stuck Queue
    .summary = Monday, 8:10, and nothing comes out of the office printer: twenty colleagues are sending their registers and worksheets again and again. Find what blocks the queue, remove only what must go, and get the school printing again.

## Files

stuck-queue-message-ines =
    From: Ines (school office)
    Monday 19 November, 8:10

    Hello, and welcome to the school!

    I hoped your first morning would be quiet, but nothing prints. The
    office printer has been silent since Friday, and the staff keep
    sending their documents again: registers, worksheets, the letter for
    the museum trip, which must go home tonight.

    Joe says the printer itself is fine now (see his note). Everything
    goes through chalk, the little server in the office cupboard: CUPS
    runs on it, and you are in the lpadmin group, so you can manage the
    queues.

    Please don't lose the colleagues' documents: they would have to send
    them all again, and they won't be happy.

    Ines

stuck-queue-note-joe =
    Note from Joe (caretaker)

    Friday at 4 pm the office printer jammed. I switched it off to pull
    the paper out and left it off for the weekend. This morning at 7:45 I
    switched it back on: no jam, paper in, its screen says Ready.

    Joe

## Events

stuck-queue-event-queue =
    The office queue is "disabled", with a reason: cupsd stopped it
    itself. When a printer can't be reached, ErrorPolicy decides; its
    default, stop-printer, stops the queue, and jobs wait in it until
    someone enables it again. What happened on Friday? cupsd's log is
    /var/log/cups/error_log.
stuck-queue-event-error-log =
    At LogLevel warn, cupsd only writes errors and warnings, but when a
    job fails it dumps that job's last debug messages (LogDebugHistory):
    from 16:05 to 16:12 the backend tried to reach the printer, every 30
    seconds, while Joe had switched it off; then cupsd stopped the queue.
    Which job was it? lpstat -o or lpq show the jobs in order.
stuck-queue-event-jobs =
    Job 117, Sven's yearbook proofs: 3.6 GB, at the head of the queue. The
    first job prints first: the moment the queue is enabled again, the
    office printer starts on it, and everyone else waits behind. And
    where does CUPS keep all that? (df -h, and /var/spool/cups.)
stuck-queue-event-disk =
    /var is almost full: CUPS keeps each job's document in its spool,
    /var/spool/cups (d00117-001 for job 117's first document, c00117 for
    its control file). Full, it would refuse new jobs.
stuck-queue-event-cancel-all =
    Ouch: cancel -a cancelled every job, the colleagues' as well. They
    are sending them again, one by one, and Ines is getting the emails.
    cancel takes a job's id: only the runaway job had to go.
stuck-queue-event-hand-rm =
    A file removed by hand from /var/spool/cups: cupsd doesn't know. For
    it, the job still exists and still has its document; it will find out
    when the job tries to print, and abort it ("Aborting job because it
    has no files."). The spool belongs to cupsd: go through it (cancel,
    cancel -x), never around it.
stuck-queue-event-edited =
    printers.conf changed by hand while cupsd runs. Its first lines say
    it: DO NOT EDIT THIS FILE WHEN CUPSD IS RUNNING. cupsd keeps the queues
    in memory and writes this file when they change and when it stops; it
    reads it only when it starts.
stuck-queue-event-edit-lost =
    Your edit is gone: on stopping, cupsd wrote its queues from memory over
    printers.conf. The queue is still stopped. The commands made for it
    (cupsenable, cupsaccept, lpadmin) change cupsd's memory and the file
    together.
stuck-queue-event-restart-only =
    cupsd restarted, and the queue is still stopped: a queue's state is
    kept in printers.conf (State Stopped) and survives a restart, on
    purpose. A restart repairs nothing here: the queue has to be enabled
    (man cupsenable).
stuck-queue-event-yearbook-printing =
    The office printer is printing the yearbook proofs: { $pages } pages
    out already, of 900, and the colleagues' jobs are waiting behind them.
    Joe is running for paper. Stop that job (cancel takes its id).
stuck-queue-event-canceled =
    Job 117 is cancelled, but look at the spool: its 3.6 GB document is
    still there. CUPS keeps a finished job's documents for
    PreserveJobFiles (a day by default, for reprints). cancel -x purges
    them (man cancel).
stuck-queue-event-purged =
    Job 117 is gone, its document too: the spool is back to a few
    megabytes (sudo du -sh /var/spool/cups, df -h /var).
stuck-queue-event-enabled =
    The office queue is enabled and accepting jobs: the colleagues'
    documents are printing (lpstat -p, lpq).
stuck-queue-event-end =
    Ines: "It's printing! The trip letters are coming out. Thank you!
    Next week the new printer for the staff room arrives..."

    Well done! You finished The Stuck Queue.

    WHAT HAPPENED
    On Friday at 16:05 Sven sent the yearbook proofs at full resolution,
    900 pages and 3.6 GB, to a printer Joe had just switched off. The
    backend couldn't connect, retried for seven minutes and failed; with
    ErrorPolicy stop-printer, cupsd stopped the queue. Monday's jobs piled
    up behind the yearbook, and the spool filled /var. You read the queue
    (lpstat, lpq), the failed job's history in error_log, cancelled job
    117 alone, purged its document (cancel -x) and enabled the queue
    again (cupsenable).

    GOOD PRACTICE
    - lpstat -t first: the scheduler, the default, each queue's device,
      whether it accepts and whether it is enabled, and the jobs.
    - A "disabled" queue has a reason: read it, then error_log. At
      LogLevel warn, a failed job's debug history is still dumped there.
    - Cancel a job by its id; cancel -a is everyone's work.
    - cancel -x purges the documents a cancelled job keeps
      (PreserveJobFiles).
    - Never edit /var/spool/cups or printers.conf behind cupsd's back.
    - A restart doesn't enable a queue: cupsenable does (and cupsaccept
      for a queue that rejects jobs).
    - ErrorPolicy retry-job instead of stop-printer: the queue keeps
      trying instead of stopping (each choice has its cost).

    DOCUMENTATION
    - lpstat: https://www.cups.org/doc/man-lpstat.html
    - cancel: https://www.cups.org/doc/man-cancel.html
    - cupsenable and cupsdisable: https://www.cups.org/doc/man-cupsenable.html
    - cupsd.conf (ErrorPolicy, PreserveJobFiles): https://www.cups.org/doc/man-cupsd.conf.html
    - Debian's printing guide: https://wiki.debian.org/SystemPrinting
